import{_ as t,o as a,c as o,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.CS-cBh1g.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Set up a robot repo","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"robot-code/setup.md","filePath":"robot-code/setup.md"}'),i={name:"robot-code/setup.md"};function n(r,e,d,h,c,p){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="set-up-a-robot-repo" tabindex="-1">Set up a robot repo <a class="header-anchor" href="#set-up-a-robot-repo" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Set up a robot repo&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The robot code stays yours: any architecture, any libraries, any C++. Mayhem adds a project file, a generated header and a small C++ library, and reads the rest of your code as it is.</p><h2 id="_1-install-the-c-library" tabindex="-1">1. Install the C++ library <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-install-the-c-library" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1. Install the C++ library&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Vendordep (recommended).</strong> In WPILib VS Code: <strong>Manage Vendor Libraries → Install new libraries (online)</strong>, and paste:</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-dark github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>https://ryanabraham1.github.io/Mayhem/MayhemCpp.json</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The library is header-only. Include what you use, such as <code>#include &lt;mayhem/auto_runner.hpp&gt;</code>. Offline: unzip <code>MayhemCpp-maven.zip</code> from a release into the WPILib maven folder and copy <code>MayhemCpp.json</code> into <code>vendordeps/</code>.</p><p><strong>Or copy the headers into the repo.</strong> This works with no network, and pins the exact headers in git:</p><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-dark github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#B392F0;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">node</span><span style="--shiki-light:#9ECBFF;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> scripts/mayhem.mjs</span><span style="--shiki-light:#9ECBFF;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> vendor</span><span style="--shiki-light:#9ECBFF;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> /path/to/robot</span><span style="--shiki-light:#79B8FF;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --snapshot</span><span style="--shiki-light:#9ECBFF;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> docs/mayhem/vendor-snapshot.json</span></span></code></pre></div><p>That copies every header to <code>src/main/include/mayhem</code> and records the version and a SHA-256 of each file. Add <code>--check</code> in CI to fail when they&#39;ve drifted. Don&#39;t edit vendored headers; change Mayhem and re-vendor.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Header</th><th>Use it for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>auto_runner.hpp</code></td><td>Running an exported routine: actions, conditions, watchdogs, telemetry.</td></tr><tr><td><code>state_machine.hpp</code></td><td>Running an exported mechanism state machine.</td></tr><tr><td><code>alliance.hpp</code></td><td>Flipping a blue-origin routine for red.</td></tr><tr><td><code>pose_driver.hpp</code></td><td>Driving to one pose with a watchdog, from any loop.</td></tr><tr><td><code>pose_controller.hpp</code></td><td>The bare controller, for your own architecture.</td></tr><tr><td><code>wpilib_log.hpp</code>, <code>networktables_observer.hpp</code></td><td>Logging states and transitions so the app can replay and show them live.</td></tr><tr><td><code>wpilib.hpp</code></td><td><code>DriveToPose</code> as a command (needs the command framework).</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The full API is in the <a href="https://github.com/ryanabraham1/Mayhem/blob/main/lib-cpp/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">C++ library README</a>.</p><h2 id="_2-add-the-project-file" tabindex="-1">2. Add the project file <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-add-the-project-file" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2. Add the project file&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Save the project as <code>robot.mayhem.json</code> at the root of the repo: open the robot code folder in the app and use <strong>Settings → Agents and the project file → Save project file</strong>. The file stays linked, so app edits save back to it and edits made by agents or <code>git checkout</code> reload in the app.</p><p>Commit it. The Changes page reads autos from it, and teammates who open the folder get the same project.</p><h2 id="_3-generate-mayhemproject-h" tabindex="-1">3. Generate <code>MayhemProject.h</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-generate-mayhemproject-h" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3. Generate `MayhemProject.h`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The routines and state machines reach the robot as a generated header:</p><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-dark github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#B392F0;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">node</span><span style="--shiki-light:#9ECBFF;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> scripts/mayhem.mjs</span><span style="--shiki-light:#9ECBFF;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> cpp</span><span style="--shiki-light:#9ECBFF;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> robot.mayhem.json</span><span style="--shiki-light:#79B8FF;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --out</span><span style="--shiki-light:#9ECBFF;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> src/main/include/MayhemProject.h</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Everything in it is <code>constexpr</code>, in <code>namespace mayhem_generated</code>: each routine (<code>routine_&lt;i&gt;::info</code>, its start pose and preview path), the <code>routines</code> array for an auto chooser, every state machine with a typed API, and the field size for alliance flipping.</p><p>Keep the header in sync in CI. This exits 1 with the command to run when the header is stale:</p><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-dark github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#B392F0;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">node</span><span style="--shiki-light:#9ECBFF;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> scripts/mayhem.mjs</span><span style="--shiki-light:#9ECBFF;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> cpp</span><span style="--shiki-light:#9ECBFF;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> robot.mayhem.json</span><span style="--shiki-light:#79B8FF;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --check</span><span style="--shiki-light:#9ECBFF;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> src/main/include/MayhemProject.h</span></span></code></pre></div><div class="tip custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">A script for the team</p><p>Put both commands in <code>tools/mayhem/export.sh</code> (write the header; <code>--check</code> for CI). The Changes page tells people to run it when the generated code is out of date, and agents use it after editing the project.</p></div><p>Pin the tools to a release in CI rather than cloning Mayhem. Each release has <code>Mayhem-suite-tools-&lt;version&gt;.zip</code> and <code>Mayhem-suite-SHA256SUMS.txt</code> to verify it.</p><h2 id="_4-wire-it-up" tabindex="-1">4. Wire it up <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-wire-it-up" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;4. Wire it up&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="/Mayhem/robot-code/autos">Running autos</a>: bind your action and signal IDs, flip for red, and start the runner.</li><li><a href="/Mayhem/robot-code/state-machines">State machines</a>: describe mechanism machines in <code>tools/mayhem/machines.json</code> and run them with the typed API.</li><li><a href="/Mayhem/robot-code/logging">Logging and what&#39;s deployed</a>: what to log so Logs, State machines and &quot;On the robot now&quot; have something to show.</li><li><a href="/Mayhem/robot-code/scenarios">Sim proof scenarios</a>: the regression tests that the Changes page runs.</li></ul><h2 id="what-mayhem-reads-from-your-code" tabindex="-1">What Mayhem reads from your code <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-mayhem-reads-from-your-code" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What Mayhem reads from your code&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The Changes and Robot → Values pages read values straight from your headers, so keep them in shapes it can read:</p><ul><li>Named constants at namespace or class scope in <code>src/main/include/**/*.h</code>, with units in the literal (<code>80_A</code>), the type, the name (<code>kTimeoutSeconds</code>) or <code>[unit]</code> in the doc comment.</li><li>CAN IDs as named constants (<code>kLeftMotorId</code>, or an array with a comment per entry).</li><li>One CTRE configuration function per motor group (<code>RightMotorConfigs()</code>, <code>MotorConfigs()</code>).</li><li>State machine transitions in <code>tools/mayhem/machines.json</code>, not only in C++.</li></ul><p>Values in a <code>sim</code> namespace or named <code>kSim…</code> are treated as simulation-only. Anything else that reaches the robot is still compared file by file and listed as <strong>Other code</strong>.</p>',28)])])}const u=t(i,[["render",n]]);export{m as __pageData,u as default};
